ent  at which  you separate your chromosomes and  there's no going back whatever you end  up with in your new  cell is what you're stuck with so  um the process of segregating  chromosomes on the spindle  is similar very similar across many  different organisms  the spindle itself shown here in green  in all of the pictures  can look very different depending on the  organism everything from humans  to sea urchins to maize all use a  network of microtubules but they can  arrange this  structure in very different ways you can  see the sea urchin spindle  has a dramatic amount of astral  microtubules facing away  whereas both human and maize have most  of their microtubules  in this football structure and all of  them have their chromosomes aligned in  the middle  in metaphase so again as i mentioned  this process of building a spindle and  attaching your chromosomes to it is  critically important  um you hopefully will align your  chromosomes and split them such that you  end up with the  proper number of chromosomes after  anaphase  but sometimes this process goes wrong in  this picture shown here  you have a chromosome attaching both  sides of the chromosome  either both chromatids to one pole or  you can have other situations  where chromosomes simply aren't attached  at all and so when you make mistakes  um in attaching your chromosomes you end  up with this situation of aneuploidy  where you have  either missing or extra numbers of  chromosomes in your cells  in my graduate studies i focused heavily  on this attachment question about how  chromosomes attached to the spindle  through a structure called the  kinetochore  and then as i moved into my postdoc i've  become increasingly interested in the  spindle itself  you can have mistakes in how the spindle  is assembled so in this picture here  we have a spindle that has has too many  poles a correct spindle is bipolar  and here in this image we have a  multi-polar spindle specifically a  tri-polar with three diffe